Barrier is a synchronization construct where a set of processes synchronize globally. Let the number of processes be three and S be a binary semaphore. Consider the following implementation:

void barrier(void) {
1:  P(S);
2:  process_arrived++;
3:  V(S);
4:  while (process_arrived != 3);
5:  P(S);
6:  process_left++;
7:  if (process_left == 3) {
8:  process_arrived = 0;
9: process_left = 0;
10: }
11: V(S);
}

The variables process_arrived and process_left are shared and initialized to zero.

Which one of the following rectifies the problem in the implementation?

(a) Lines 6 to 10 are simply replaced by process_arrived--
(b) At the beginning of the barrier the first process to enter waits until process_arrived becomes zero before proceeding to execute P(S)
(c) Context switch is disabled at the beginning of the barrier and re-enabled at the end
(d) The variable process_left is made private instead of shared

